---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BlogPost from '../../components/BlogPost.astro';
import { countries, getReferralUrl } from '../../data/countries';
import { posts } from '../../data/posts';

const country = countries.find((c) => c.slug === 'uk')!;
const referralUrl = getReferralUrl(country);
const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === '650-free-supercharging-miles-value')!;

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: post.title,
  description: post.description,
  datePublished: post.date,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'UK Tesla Model 3 owner' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Tesla Referral Code UK',
    url: 'https://teslareferralcode.co/',
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: `https://teslareferralcode.co/blog/${post.slug}/`,
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | Tesla Referral Code UK`}
  description={post.description}
  country={country}
  canonical={`https://teslareferralcode.co/blog/${post.slug}/`}
  jsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <BlogPost title={post.title} date={post.date} readTime={post.readTime}>
    <p>
      650 free Supercharging miles is the headline Tesla referral perk in the
      UK for Model 3 and Model Y buyers. It sounds nice and round, but what is
      it actually worth? Below is the maths, the real-world driving range it
      covers, and a couple of ways to get more out of the credit.
    </p>

    <h2>The cash value: roughly £120</h2>
    <p>
      Tesla Supercharger pricing in the UK varies by site and by time of day,
      but a reasonable average in 2026 is around 45 to 50 pence per kWh at
      off-peak rates, and 65 to 70 pence per kWh at peak. A typical Tesla
      consumes about 25 kWh per 100 miles in mixed driving. That works out to
      between 11 and 18 pence per mile of charging cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      650 miles of charging credit therefore lands somewhere between £70 (off
      peak) and £115 (peak). In practice most people charge at a mix of times
      and prices, so the credit is worth around £100 to £120 in real money. Not
      life-changing, but enough to cover several long trips.
    </p>

    <h2>What 650 miles of charging actually covers</h2>
    <p>
      650 miles of free charging is not the same as 650 miles of free driving.
      You will only use a Supercharger when you cannot charge at home, which is
      mostly on road trips. Here is what the credit covers in real journeys:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>London to Edinburgh and back</strong> (around 820 miles round trip). One full Supercharger stop each way, around 200 miles of credit used.</li>
      <li><strong>London to the Lake District for a long weekend.</strong> One charge there, one back. Around 150 miles used.</li>
      <li><strong>Manchester to Cornwall return.</strong> Two or three charges total, around 250 miles.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In other words, 650 miles is enough for two or three big UK road trips
      before you need to start paying. For drivers who mostly charge at home,
      that can easily cover the first full year of long-distance driving.
    </p>

    <h2>Tip: combine with off-peak home charging</h2>
    <p>
      The cheapest way to run a Tesla in the UK is to charge at home overnight
      on a smart EV tariff. Octopus Go, Intelligent Octopus, and similar
      tariffs offer rates around 7 to 8 pence per kWh between roughly midnight
      and 5am. At that rate, a full Tesla charge costs about £4 to £5 for 250
      miles of driving.
    </p>
    <p>
      Save the free Supercharging credit for genuine long trips where you need
      to top up away from home. Do not burn it on a Supercharger session two
      miles from your house just because it is technically free.
    </p>

    <h2>How to track your credit</h2>
    <p>
      Once you take delivery of your Tesla, the Supercharging credit appears in
      the Tesla app under the Charging tab. It shows up as a balance in miles,
      and Tesla deducts from it automatically every time you Supercharge until
      it is used up. You will get an email when the balance is running low.
    </p>

    <h2>Do the miles expire?</h2>
    <p>
      Tesla has not published an expiry date for referral Supercharging miles
      in the UK. Owners who received credit in 2021 still report having the
      balance on their account. That said, Tesla can change the rules at any
      time, so do not stockpile the credit indefinitely.
    </p>

    <h2>Is the perk actually worth using a referral code for?</h2>
    <p>
      Compared to the price of the car, £100 to £120 of charging credit is a
      small percentage. But the price you pay is identical whether you use a
      referral code or not, so there is no reason to skip it. It is genuinely
      free money for new buyers. The only mistake is forgetting to apply the
      code before you place the order.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href={referralUrl} rel="nofollow" class="inline-block mt-2">
        <span class="btn-primary">Use the referral link</span>
      </a>
    </p>

    <h2>Read next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/blog/how-tesla-referral-programme-works-uk/">How the Tesla referral programme works in the UK</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/tesla-referral-code-at-tesla-store/">How to use a Tesla referral code at a Tesla store</a></li>
      <li><a href="/">Back to the main page and FAQ</a></li>
    </ul>
  </BlogPost>
</Layout>
